From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <DCHEM88@hotmail.it>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 02:47:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mb/lenovo/haswell: Handle the EC events the stock firmware
 handles

The devicetrees of the W541 and the T440p enable EC events that this
DSDT has no _Qxx method for (h8.c writes eventN_enable to EC 0x10+N),
so the OS drops them. The Lenovo firmware (W541 2.42, GNET94WW; T440p
2.56, GLETA2WW; their DSDTs decompiled) reports several of them to the
OS as HKEY events, which thinkpad_acpi acts on: battery and sensor
alarms ("THERMAL ALARM: battery is too hot!", a netlink event for
userspace), dock and undock. Report them the same way, with the same
codes, through RHK and RHKX:

- 0x18: hotkey 0x1009, when the OS enables it, as the stock firmware;
- 0x3f (W541 only, its numeric keypad): NumLock, 0x6000;
- 0x40: thermal alarm. Notify the thermal zone, and report 0x6022 if
  the EC's alarm level (stock TSL2, EC 0x8a bits 0-6) is not zero. It
  is a critical level: when it is not zero and no hotkey driver runs,
  the stock _TMP returns 128 C, which shuts the machine down, while the
  CPU throttling of the stock _Q40 follows another level (TSL0);
- 0x45: dock, 0x4010 or 0x4011 from EC 0x48 bit 5 (stock DOCD), read
  first and reported 100 ms later, as the stock firmware does;
- 0x46, 0x4e, 0x4f: battery alarms, 0x6012, 0x6011 and 0x6012.

The two stock DSDTs handle these events identically, their EC field
maps are the same, and both devicetrees enable them except 0x3f (the
T440p has event7_enable 0x01 and no keypad): only _Q3F is under
CONFIG(BOARD_LENOVO_THINKPAD_W541).

Add the software mute handshake too. thinkpad_acpi reads HAUM (EC 0x03
bits 5-6, what the mute and volume buttons do in the EC) and calls
SAUM (2), "only keys". Without them it creates the "ThinkPad Console
Audio Control" ALSA mixer on top of the EC; with them mute is left to
the codec, as with the stock firmware. config3 0xc4 of both devicetrees
already sets HAUM to 2 at boot and on resume, so SAUM (2) does not
change the EC. The stock SAUM has no Return and works through the
implicit return of ACPICA; this one returns HAUM, which thinkpad_acpi
checks. In software mute mode thinkpad_acpi also clears the EC mute
bit, EC 0x30 bit 6, at load and on every resume (volume_set_mute(false)
for the mute-only Lenovo models). Here that bit is ALMT, which SSMS sets
for the mute LED; the stock SSMS goes through an SMI instead. The
mute_led subdriver of thinkpad_acpi comes after the volume one, though:
on resume mute_led_resume() writes the LED state back through SSMS right
after the clear, and at load the LED is registered after it, so that
the audio-mute trigger sets it again (the hardware test checks both).
thinkpad_acpi.software_mute=0 keeps the old behaviour.

Left out, with the reason in ec.asl: 0x1e, 0x6b-0x6f and 0x77 (no stock
handler either), 0x41 (stock reports 0x7000, "radio switch changed",
but its WLSW is a constant, so the event changes nothing there; ours
returns EC 0x48 bit 1, GSTS, and on 0x7000 thinkpad_acpi would read it
at runtime and hard-block the radios if it reads 0, which nobody has
checked, on boards without a radio switch), 0x43 (a stock SMI only),
0x48, 0x49 and on the W541 0x5b (EC requests for a CPU limit: the stock
_PPC follows the adapter wattage, the batteries and EC power limit
registers, ours does not use the EC; this needs its own change and a
test with a 65 W or 90 W adapter), 0x2c, 0x2d, 0x38 and 0x44 (Ultrabay:
no bay device here), 0x70, 0x72, 0x73 and 0x74 (sticky Fn and Fn+Esc:
thinkpad_acpi ignores 0x6005 and 0x6060, stock calls its SMI handler,
0x6060 needs GMKS).

HKEY keeps one pending event (BTN), where the stock firmware keeps one
per class, so two events in a row before the OS reads them lose the
first. The events added here are rare, and the sticky Fn events, which
would follow hotkeys closely, stay out for that reason.

TEST=Host build of the DSDT as coreboot does (cpp with the W541 ci-150
nri and mrc config, and a T440p config derived from it, then iasl -we):
0 errors and 0 warnings, the same remarks as before, and the AML
disassembles without ACPI warnings. A script lists the events each
devicetree enables against the _Qxx of the DSDT: 0x18, 0x3f, 0x40, 0x45,
0x46, 0x4e and 0x4f now handled on the W541, the same but 0x3f on the
T440p, the rest as listed above. An acpiexec test SSDT runs the methods
on both DSDTs, 22 checks: SAUM writes only bits 5-6 of EC 0x03 and
returns the mode, ignores values over 3; each _Qxx reports its code,
nothing while HKEY is off, 0x1009 only when unmasked; DKST and TSLC
read the right bits; there is no _Q41. Still to test on the W541:
- docking and undocking log "docked into hotplug port replicator" and
  "undocked ...";
- _Q4E, _Q4F and _Q40 called through acpi_call log the thermal alarms
  with no ACPI error;
- the real 0x40 events: EC 0x88-0x8b (ec_sys, read only) at idle and
  during 10 minutes of stress-ng, on AC and on battery, with the EC
  queries counted (dynamic debug of drivers/acpi/ec.c, or the EC GPE
  0x11 in /sys/firmware/acpi/interrupts) and no THERMAL line in dmesg;
  if EC 0x8a reads non-zero at normal temperatures, report only the
  Notify;
- thinkpad_acpi no longer logs "Console audio control enabled",
  /proc/asound/cards has no ThinkPad EC card, the mute key and its LED
  work; muted, the LED stays lit after S3 and after a reload of
  thinkpad_acpi;
- macOS with OpenCore and Windows (with the Lenovo drivers) boot
  without ACPI errors and without thermal warnings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/acpi/ec.asl | 111 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 111 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/acpi/ec.asl b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/acpi/ec.asl
index 05990f6bd1..acd6f967c2 100644
--- a/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/acpi/ec.asl
+++ b/src/mainboard/lenovo/haswell/acpi/ec.asl
@@ -7,6 +7,14 @@ Scope (EC)
 {
 	Field (ERAM, ByteAcc, NoLock, Preserve)
 	{
+		/*
+		 * What the mute and volume buttons do in the EC, read by
+		 * thinkpad_acpi (TP_EC_MUTE_BTN_*): config3 of the devicetree
+		 * sets 2, where they are only keys.
+		 */
+		Offset (0x03),
+		    , 5,
+		HAUM, 2,
 		/*
 		 * Wake cause. The OEM firmware reads 16 bits, but the bits
 		 * that _L1D tests are all in the first byte: one byte is
@@ -14,6 +22,109 @@ Scope (EC)
 		 */
 		Offset (0x36),
 		HWAC, 8,
+		Offset (0x48),
+		    , 5,
+		DKST, 1,	/* Docked (DOCD of the OEM firmware) */
+		Offset (0x8a),
+		TSLC, 7,	/* Thermal alarm level (TSL2 of the OEM firmware) */
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Software mute, for thinkpad_acpi: it sets the button mode to 2 and
+	 * checks the result, then leaves mute to the codec. The OEM SAUM has
+	 * no Return; this one returns the mode, which thinkpad_acpi checks.
+	 */
+	Method (SAUM, 1, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		If (Arg0 <= 3) {
+			HAUM = Arg0
+		}
+		Return (HAUM)
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The EC events that the devicetree enables and that the OEM firmware
+	 * reports to the OS, as HKEY events with the OEM codes (thinkpad_acpi
+	 * names them). The OEM DSDTs of the W541 and of the T440p handle them
+	 * alike, and both devicetrees enable them, except the NumLock event.
+	 *
+	 * Left out:
+	 * - 0x1e, 0x6b-0x6f and 0x77: no handler in the OEM firmware either;
+	 * - 0x41 (0x7000, radio switch changed): the OEM WLSW is a constant,
+	 *   so the event changes nothing there, while this WLSW returns EC
+	 *   0x48 bit 1 (GSTS). On 0x7000 thinkpad_acpi reads WLSW again and
+	 *   hard-blocks the radios if it is 0, which nothing has checked on
+	 *   these boards, and neither has a radio switch;
+	 * - 0x43: an SMI to the OEM firmware only (UCMS 0x18);
+	 * - 0x48, 0x49 and, on the W541, 0x5b: limits the EC asks for the
+	 *   CPU. The OEM _PPC follows the adapter wattage, the batteries and
+	 *   the EC power limit registers; this _PPC does not use the EC;
+	 * - 0x2c, 0x2d, 0x38 and 0x44: insertion and ejection in the
+	 *   Ultrabay, for which this DSDT has no bay device;
+	 * - 0x70, 0x72 and 0x73 (sticky Fn state, 0x6005) and 0x74 (Fn+Esc,
+	 *   0x6060): thinkpad_acpi ignores them, the OEM firmware also calls
+	 *   its SMI handler for them, and on 0x6060 thinkpad_acpi calls GMKS,
+	 *   which this HKEY does not have. HKEY keeps one event (BTN), and a
+	 *   sticky Fn event right after a hotkey would replace it.
+	 */
+
+	/* Hotkey 0x1009 (Fn+F9 in the thinkpad_acpi key map), if enabled */
+	Method (_Q18, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		^HKEY.RHK (0x09)
+	}
+
+#if CONFIG(BOARD_LENOVO_THINKPAD_W541)
+	/* NumLock of the numeric keypad (0x6000); the key itself comes too */
+	Method (_Q3F, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		^HKEY.RHKX (0x6000)
+	}
+#endif
+
+	/*
+	 * Thermal alarm: the thermal zone reads the temperature again, and an
+	 * alarm level is "a sensor reports something is extremely hot" for
+	 * thinkpad_acpi (0x6022). It is a critical level: when it is not zero
+	 * and no hotkey driver runs, the OEM _TMP returns 128 C, a shutdown.
+	 */
+	Method (_Q40, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		Notify (\_TZ.THM0, 0x80)
+		If (TSLC) {
+			^HKEY.RHKX (0x6022)
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Docked (0x4010) or undocked (0x4011). As the OEM firmware: the dock
+	 * bit is read first, the event is reported 100 ms later.
+	 */
+	Method (_Q45, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		Local0 = DKST
+		Sleep (100)
+		If (Local0) {
+			^HKEY.RHKX (0x4010)
+		} Else {
+			^HKEY.RHKX (0x4011)
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* Battery alarms: too hot (0x6011), extremely hot (0x6012) */
+	Method (_Q4E, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		^HKEY.RHKX (0x6011)
+	}
+
+	Method (_Q46, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		^HKEY.RHKX (0x6012)
+	}
+
+	Method (_Q4F, 0, NotSerialized)
+	{
+		^HKEY.RHKX (0x6012)
 	}
 }
 
